{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nYou are given an array a consisting of n integers, and additionally an integer m. You have to choose some sequence of indices b1,\u2009b2,\u2009...,\u2009bk (1\u2009\u2264\u2009b1\u2009&lt;\u2009b2\u2009&lt;\u2009...\u2009&lt;\u2009bk\u2009\u2264\u2009n) in such a way that the value of  is maximized. Chosen sequence can be empty.Print the maximum possible value of .\n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 3 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\nimport bisect\n\nn, m = [int(i) for i in input().split()]\nnums = [int(i) for i in input().split()]\n\nhalf = n // 2\nleft_nums = nums[:half]\nright_nums = nums[half:]\n\ndef enumerate_sums_modulo(sublist, modulus):\n    results = []\n    k = len(sublist)\n    for mask in range(1 << k):\n        acc = 0\n        for idx in range(k):\n            if mask & (1 << idx):\n                acc += sublist[idx]\n        results.append(acc % modulus)\n    return sorted(results)\n\nleft_results = enumerate_sums_modulo(left_nums, m)\nright_results = enumerate_sums_modulo(right_nums, m)\n\nmax_val = 0\nfor left_val in left_results:\